actors: Browser | Client app | Lab IdP

step: The user asks to log in
msg: Browser -> Client app: click "Log in"
text: The user clicks "Log in" on the client app. At this point the app knows
  nothing about them, and it never will see their password.

step: The app invents a secret and shows only its fingerprint
msg: Client app -> Browser: redirect + code_challenge
note: Client app: keeps code_verifier secret
text: The app generates a random `code_verifier`, hashes it into a
  `code_challenge`, and redirects the browser to the IdP carrying only the
  challenge. The verifier stays on the app's side.

  Notice that someone who intercepts this redirect learns the hash, not the
  secret.

step: The browser carries the request to the IdP
msg: Browser -> Lab IdP: GET /authorize?code_challenge=...
note: Lab IdP: stores code_challenge
text: The IdP stores the challenge next to the pending request. It will compare
  it against whatever shows up at the token endpoint later.

step: The user authenticates at the IdP, not the app
msg: Lab IdP -> Browser: login + consent, redirect with ?code=
text: Login and consent happen on the IdP's own pages. The IdP then redirects
  back with a short-lived, single-use `code`. A code on its own is useless;
  that is the point of the next two steps.

step: The code lands on the app's callback
msg: Browser -> Client app: GET /callback?code=...
text: The code travelled through the browser, so treat it as exposed. If an
  attacker grabbed it here, what would stop them from redeeming it?

step: The app proves it started the flow
msg: Client app -> Lab IdP: POST /token (code + verifier)
text: The app posts the code together with the original `code_verifier`. The IdP
  hashes the verifier and compares it with the stored challenge. A thief with
  only the code fails this check, which is exactly the lab's PKCE-mismatch
  rejection.

step: Tokens come back over the direct channel
msg: Lab IdP -> Client app: ID token + access token
text: Tokens only ever travel on the app-to-IdP request, never through the
  browser's address bar. Open the trace panel and find these seven messages
  in the live trace.
